{"plan": "Grades 5-8, deep: a noon sunbeam of fixed width landing on the ground (narrow bright patch vs wide thin patch), with month, place (New York / Melbourne) and an axis-tilt switch, a daylight-hours bar, and a plot of noon sun height over the year.", "source": "const MONTHS = ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June", "July", "August", "September", "October", "November", "December"];
const SHORT = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"];
const PLACES = { "New York (north)": { name: "New York", lat: 40.7, faces: "south" }, "Melbourne (south)": { name: "Melbourne", lat: -37.8, faces: "north" } };
const TILT = 23.44;
const GY = 290;      // ground line
const CX = 360;      // where the beam lands
const R = 230;       // sun path radius
const W = 60;        // beam width in px = 1 m
const RAD = Math.PI / 180;

const dayOf = (month) => 15.2 + 30.44 * month;
const declination = (month, tilt) => (tilt ? TILT * Math.sin((2 * Math.PI * (284 + dayOf(month))) / 365) : 0);
const noonHeight = (month, lat, tilt) => 90 - Math.abs(lat - declination(month, tilt));
const dayLength = (month, lat, tilt) => {
  const c = -Math.tan(lat * RAD) * Math.tan(declination(month, tilt) * RAD);
  return (2 * Math.acos(Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, c)))) / RAD / 15;
};

figure({
  title: "Sunlight and the seasons",
  icon: "☀️",
  level: "5-8",
  depth: "deep",
  mode: "flat",
  size: [800, 380],
  question: "Why is summer hotter than winter?",
  caption:
    "Earth's axis is tilted, so in summer the noon sun climbs high. A high sun's beam lands on a small patch of ground, so each square metre gets concentrated sunlight, and the day is longer. In winter the low sun spreads the same beam thin, for fewer hours.",
  leavesOut:
    "Air, oceans and clouds store and move heat, so the hottest weeks come about a month after the longest day. Earth's distance from the sun changes a little (closest in January), but that is not what makes the seasons.",
  try: "Find New York's lowest noon sun. Is Earth near or far from the sun that month?",
  controls: [
    { id: "month", label: "Month", min: 0, max: 11, step: 1, value: 6, format: (v) => "mid-" + MONTHS[Math.round(v)] },
    { id: "place", label: "Where you are", type: "choice", options: ["New York (north)", "Melbourne (south)"], value: "New York (north)" },
    { id: "tilt", label: "Earth's axis tilted 23.4°", type: "toggle", value: true },
  ],
  tour: true,

  model(s) {
    const place = PLACES[s.place];
    const alt = noonHeight(s.month, place.lat, s.tilt);
    const altShown = Math.round(alt);
    const patch = Math.round((1 / Math.sin(alt * RAD)) * 100) / 100;
    const strength = Math.round(100 / patch);
    const hours = dayLength(s.month, place.lat, s.tilt);
    const dist = Math.round(149.6 * (1 - 0.0167 * Math.cos((2 * Math.PI * (dayOf(s.month) - 3)) / 365)));
    const june = noonHeight(5.2, place.lat, s.tilt);
    const dec = noonHeight(11.2, place.lat, s.tilt);
    return { place, alt, altShown, patch, strength, hours, hoursShown: Math.round(hours), dist, june, dec, monthName: MONTHS[Math.round(s.month)] };
  },
  goal: (m, s) => s.tilt && s.place === "New York (north)" && Math.abs(s.month - 11) < 0.3,
  readout: (m, s) => [
    `${m.place.name}, mid-${m.monthName}: the noon sun is ${m.altShown}° above the horizon.${s.tilt ? "" : " With no tilt it is the same every month: no seasons."}`,
    `The 1 m wide beam spreads over ${m.patch.toFixed(2)} m of ground: 100 ÷ ${m.patch.toFixed(2)} = ${m.strength} units of sunlight per metre.`,
    `Daylight: about ${m.hoursShown} hours.  Distance to the sun: ${m.dist} million km (${m.dist < 150 ? "nearer" : "farther"} than average).`,
  ],

  draw(m, s, f) {
    const a = m.alt * RAD;
    const sx = CX - R * Math.cos(a);
    const sy = GY - R * Math.sin(a);
    const n = [Math.sin(a), -Math.cos(a)];
    const half = W / 2 / Math.sin(a);
    const onArc = (deg, extra) => [CX - (R + extra) * Math.cos(deg * RAD), GY - (R + extra) * Math.sin(deg * RAD)];

    // beam: parallel rays from the sun to the ground
    const beamPoly = [
      [sx + n[0] * W / 2, sy + n[1] * W / 2],
      [CX + half, GY],
      [CX - half, GY],
      [sx - n[0] * W / 2, sy - n[1] * W / 2],
    ];
    const rays = [-0.3, 0, 0.3].map((k) => {
      const ox = n[0] * W * k, oy = n[1] * W * k;
      return f.line(sx + ox, sy + oy, CX + k * 2 * half, GY, { stroke: "warm", width: 1.2, opacity: 0.55, dash: "6 6" });
    });
    const mid = [(sx + CX) / 2, (sy + GY) / 2];
    const p1 = [mid[0] + n[0] * W / 2, mid[1] + n[1] * W / 2];
    const p2 = [mid[0] - n[0] * W / 2, mid[1] - n[1] * W / 2];
    const lab = [mid[0] + n[0] * (W / 2 + 22), mid[1] + n[1] * (W / 2 + 22)];

    // solstice markers on the sun's path
    const marks = s.tilt
      ? [["June", m.june], ["Dec", m.dec]].map(([name, deg]) => {
          const [x, y] = onArc(deg, 0);
          const [tx, ty] = onArc(deg, 26);
          return [f.circle(x, y, 4, { fill: "lo" }), f.text(tx, ty + 5, name, { size: 13, fill: "mid" })];
        })
      : [];

    // daylight bar (24 h, noon in the middle)
    const bx = 20, bw = 210, by = 34;
    const dayStart = bx + ((12 - m.hours / 2) / 24) * bw;
    const daylight = [
      f.text(bx, by - 8, `Daylight: about ${m.hoursShown} hours`, { anchor: "start", size: 14 }),
      f.rect(bx, by, bw, 14, { r: 7, fill: "night" }),
      f.rect(dayStart, by, (m.hours / 24) * bw, 14, { r: 7, fill: "sun", glow: true }),
      [["6 am", 6], ["noon", 12], ["6 pm", 18]].map(([t, h]) => f.text(bx + (h / 24) * bw, by + 30, t, { size: 12, fill: "mid" })),
    ];

    const other = s.place === "New York (north)" ? -37.8 : 40.7;

    return [
      f.rect(0, 0, 580, GY, { fill: "sky" }),
      f.arc(CX, GY, R, -85, -5, { stroke: "lo", width: 1.5, dash: "3 6", fill: "none" }),
      marks,
      f.part("Sunbeam", [f.poly(beamPoly, { fill: "warm-soft", opacity: 0.85 }), rays], "A 1 m wide bundle of sunlight. It always carries the same amount of light."),
      f.line(p1[0], p1[1], p2[0], p2[1], { stroke: "ink", width: 1.5 }),
      f.text(lab[0], lab[1] + 5, "1 m", { size: 14, weight: 600 }),
      f.part("Sun", f.circle(sx, sy, 34, { fill: "sun", glow: true }), "Its height at noon depends on the month and on where you are."),
      f.rect(0, GY, 580, 90, { fill: "earth" }),
      f.ground(0, 580, GY),
      f.part(
        "Sunlit patch",
        [
          f.ellipse(CX, GY + 2, half + 10, 9, { fill: "warm-soft", opacity: 0.8 }),
          f.rect(CX - half, GY - 3, 2 * half, 8, { r: 4, fill: "warm", opacity: 0.25 + (0.75 * m.strength) / 100, glow: m.strength > 80 }),
        ],
        "Where the beam lands. Smaller and brighter when the sun is high; wider and dimmer when it is low."
      ),
      f.line(CX - half, GY + 16, CX + half, GY + 16, { stroke: "ink", width: 1.5 }),
      f.line(CX - half, GY + 10, CX - half, GY + 22, { stroke: "ink", width: 1.5 }),
      f.line(CX + half, GY + 10, CX + half, GY + 22, { stroke: "ink", width: 1.5 }),
      f.text(CX, GY + 40, `${m.patch.toFixed(2)} m of ground`, { size: 14, weight: 600 }),
      f.text(14, GY - 10, `facing ${m.place.faces}`, { anchor: "start", size: 13, fill: "mid" }),
      f.text(566, GY + 70, `${m.place.name}, ${Math.abs(m.place.lat).toFixed(0)}°${m.place.lat > 0 ? "N" : "S"}`, { anchor: "end", size: 14, fill: "mid" }),
      f.part("Daylight bar", daylight, "How many of the day's 24 hours the sun is up. More hours of sun means more warming."),
      f.part(
        "Year graph",
        f.plot(645, 44, 140, 236, {
          x: [0, 11],
          y: [0, 90],
          xTick: (v) => SHORT[Math.round(v)] || "",
          xTicks: 4,
          yTicks: 4,
          yTick: (v) => `${v}°`,
          yLabel: "Noon sun height",
          xLabel: "month",
          lines: [
            { fn: (x) => noonHeight(x, other, s.tilt), stroke: "lo", dash: "4 4" },
            { fn: (x) => noonHeight(x, m.place.lat, s.tilt), stroke: "accent" },
          ],
          marks: [{ x: s.month, y: m.alt, fill: "accent", drop: true }],
        }),
        "The noon sun's height through the year here (solid) and in the other city (dashed)."
      ),
    ];
  },
});
"}

